## Lore

Wolverine People are tall, powerfully built D-Bees covered in brown fur,
with dark manes streaked in lighter shades, narrow pointed faces, ears that
never stop moving and amber eyes. They look like wild animals, and some
Russians liken them to the Yeti, but they are clever hunters and canny
scouts. Males are bearded and shaggy; females are sleeker and slighter, a
little weaker, quicker, and every bit as deadly.

Most belong to the Sovietski Army. Many are taken in as children and raised
in barracks the soldiers call the Kennels, where they learn discipline,
communist ideology, scouting, tracking and war. They serve until they are too
old or fall in battle, asking little more than a cot, three meals, vodka and
the chance to fight beside comrades they regard as equals. Their noses find
fuel, explosives and demons; their howl, shriek and bellow warn the unit
across miles of tundra, and enemies often mistake it for a beast.

They fear the supernatural by instinct. Training turns that flight into
fury, and once they learn they can hurt demons with the right weapons they
relish it. Off duty they are cheerful and rowdy, fond of wrestling, darts,
knife throwing, cards and dice, and loud, bad singing.

Wild clans still roam the Russian wilderness: rougher and less civilized,
they hunt for themselves, trade furs and meat with villagers, and rarely
attack unless threatened. Wronged, they can take a terrible revenge.

## GM Notes

Also called Guard Dogs, Russian Yeti and Wolverine Men. Height: males 6 feet
6 inches to 7 feet 3 inches (1.8-2.2 m), females 6-7 feet (1.8-2.1 m).
Weight: males 200-400 lbs (90-180 kg), females 160-250 lbs (72-113 kg).
Life span 4D6+50 years; mature by 15. Females bear 1D4 young as often as
every three years, far less often in military service; wild ones tend to die
young. Experience Level 1D4 for NPCs; player characters start at level one
or two.

Alignment: typically Scrupulous (50%), Unprincipled (20%), Principled (10%),
Anarchist (10%) or other. Horror Factor 12, until people get used to them.

Habitat: mostly Sovietski territory, wherever the army sends them; wild
loners, pairs, clans and hunting bands anywhere in Russia, Siberia included,
and occasionally Scandinavia, Poland and Germany. They prefer cold
woodlands. Slave market value up to 1D4x10,000 credits for an ordinary
soldier or scout, as much as 70,000 for a seasoned fighter sold as a
gladiator, for either sex.

Allies: loyal to the Sovietski for life, and kin to the wild clans; they
feel camaraderie with soldiers, Wilderness Scouts, hunters and craftspeople,
and admire artists, poets, storytellers, musicians and dancers. Enemies:
the Sovietski''s enemies, supernatural evil and anyone who harms them or
theirs; they mistrust technology, scientists, robots, cyborgs and strange
aliens, especially those wielding odd powers, magic or machines.

A player character is usually a Sovietski soldier, which can confine the
campaign, but Black Ops and front-line postings give freedom, and a special
mission may pair one with outsiders. A deserter, a lost soldier, a veteran
or a wild-born hunter all work as well.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sovietski-wolverine-people');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sovietski-wolverine-people';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-sovietski-wolverine-people-class.sql');
